We’ll now explore permutation-based approaches for computing p-values for the observed \(\chi^2\) statistic. We have seen how the arrangement of counts, rather than the marginal percentages, determines the presence or absence of an association between the two variables. When permuting the data, our null should disrupt any associations by preserving either row or column totals and shuffling the other.


Repurposing code we have explored already to perform permutation, run a map with 10,000 random permutations for each of the survey locations separately. Use the chi_sq function to calculate the \(\chi^2\) statistic for each iteration to construct null distributions for each survey location. Will the p-value be one- or two-tailed?
Find the p-values associated with each \(\chi^2\) value per surveyed location.


In a 2x2 scenario, we can compute the an effect size by finding the odds within each group (say PCs and Macs) of seeing each group of the other variable (iPhones and Android devices). Watch Odds and Log(odds), Clearly Explained!!!


In this scenario, we can then summarize one of the odds as the fraction of counts in each column:

\[
odds_{iPhone \ among \ Mac \ users} = \frac{n_{iPhone \ and \ Mac}}{n_{Android \ and \ Mac}}
\]

What is the other odds in the pair?
What do you hypothesize a priori the OR in the expected_preferences of a user preferring an iPhone given a preference of a Mac over the odds of preferring an iPhone given a preference of a PC would be? Calculate the OR and compare it against your hypothesis.
Now compute the ORs for the three survey locations: Atlanta, Seattle and Washington D.C.
